# Converting std::string to std::vector in C++

There are several ways to convert a `std::string` to a `std::vector`, depending on what type of vector you need.

## 1. To `std::vector<char>`

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::string str = "Hello";

// Method 1: Using iterator constructor
std::vector<char> vec1(str.begin(), str.end());

// Method 2: Using assign
std::vector<char> vec2;
vec2.assign(str.begin(), str.end());

// Method 3: Using copy
std::vector<char> vec3(str.size());
std::copy(str.begin(), str.end(), vec3.begin());
```

## 2. To `std::vector<unsigned char>` (for byte data)

```cpp
std::string str = "Hello";

std::vector<unsigned char> vec(str.begin(), str.end());
```

## 3. To `std::vector<std::string>` (split by delimiter)

```cpp
#include <sstream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

std::string str = "one,two,three";
std::vector<std::string> vec;
std::stringstream ss(str);
std::string token;

while (std::getline(ss, token, ',')) {
    vec.push_back(token);
}
// vec = {"one", "two", "three"}
```

## Quick Comparison

| Method | Use Case |
|--------|----------|
| Iterator constructor | Simplest, most common |
| `assign()` | When vector already exists |
| Split with delimiter | When parsing CSV-like data |

## Example Output

```cpp
std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<char> vec(str.begin(), str.end());

for (char c : vec) {
    std::cout << c << " ";
}
// Output: H e l l o
```

The **iterator constructor** method is generally the most concise and preferred approach for simple conversions.

